# LibreWolf about-config.patch  (Android)
# Owner: LW-M4-09 ("Enable about:config on release builds")
#
# Upstream file touched
# ----------------------
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/gecko/GeckoProvider.kt
#
# One hunk, one call argument.
#
#
# READ THIS FIRST: WHICH TREE THE LINE NUMBERS BELOW COME FROM
# -------------------------------------------------------------
# The Android build tracks Firefox **ESR**, not the desktop release. ./version
# is 153.0.4 and ./version.android is 153.0esr, and the ESR tarball unpacks to
# `firefox-153.0/`, NOT `firefox-153.0esr/` (Makefile:201-206). The tree sitting
# in the repo root as `firefox-153.0.4/` is therefore the DESKTOP tree and is the
# wrong file to read for anything in patches/android/.
#
# This is not pedantry, it has already cost this patch two rounds of wrong
# citations. The two trees really do differ in the file this patch is about:
#   mobile/shared/chrome/geckoview/config.xhtml
#     ESR (153.0, what we ship)  md5 ae29fd773b4d8de9db0660cde64a4af0 -- inline
#                                onclick=/onload= handlers, no ids on the buttons
#     desktop 153.0.4            md5 bd20ae6c4d1008a5c9bb3388c1ffd95a -- refactored
#                                to id="pref-toggle-button" / "copy-pref-name" and
#                                addEventListener
# The APK's assets/omni.ja carries the ESR one, byte-identical (checked by
# unzipping omni.ja out of the built release APK and diffing). So ids like
# `#pref-toggle-button` DO NOT EXIST in what we ship, and a previous "correction"
# to this header that renumbered config.js from the desktop tree made it wrong.
# Every line number below is from the ESR tree.
#
#
# What LibreWolf wants instead, and why
# --------------------------------------
# Upstream reads, at pristine ESR GeckoProvider.kt:123,
#
#     .aboutConfigEnabled(Config.channel.isBeta || Config.channel.isNightlyOrDebug)
#
# and Config.channel is BuildConfig.BUILD_TYPE, so about:config is ON for the
# debug, nightly and beta build types and OFF for `release` -- the only build type
# LibreWolf ships. The argument lands in GeckoRuntimeSettings.mAboutConfig, a
# Pref<Boolean> for `general.aboutConfig.enable` (GeckoRuntimeSettings.java:768),
# and nsAboutRedirector.cpp:285-288 returns NS_ERROR_NOT_AVAILABLE for
# about:config when that pref is false. LibreWolf's entire configuration story is
# prefs, so a shipping build with no pref editor is not shippable. Hence `true`.
#
# Note that RuntimeSettings.Pref.addToBundle() (RuntimeSettings.java:157-158)
# writes `mIsSet ? mValue : defaultValue`, i.e. GeckoView commits this pref on
# EVERY startup whether or not the call site touched it, and the Java-side
# defaultValue is `false`. So the release build does not merely fail to enable
# about:config, it actively pushes `general.aboutConfig.enable = false` over
# GeckoView:SetDefaultPrefs after autoconfig has run. That is why a `defaultPref`
# in librewolf.cfg would NOT have been an equivalent fix (landmine L2), and why
# the change has to be at the Kotlin call site.
#
# (Gecko's own StaticPrefList.yaml default for this pref is `true`, and
# nsAboutRedirector's GetBool falls back to `true` as well. Neither helps: the
# call site is unconditional, so GeckoView overwrites the default branch on every
# startup with whatever that argument evaluates to.)
#
# Hard-coded `true` rather than a Settings/Nimbus lookup, for the same reasons
# LW-M5-02 gives for isolatedProcessEnabled: a constant cannot be lowered by a
# remote experiment, by a Fenix settings write, or by an upstream flip of an FML
# default on the next rebase. There is no Fenix UI for this setting on any
# channel, so nothing is made inconsistent by ignoring one.
#
# A user can still turn it off from inside about:config itself by setting
# general.aboutConfig.enable = false on the USER branch, which then wins over the
# default this call site commits and locks them out until the profile is reset.
# That is upstream desktop behaviour too, and it is deliberate: LibreWolf does
# not lockPref this one, because locking it would take the choice away.
#
#
# THE WARNING INTERSTITIAL: THERE IS NONE ON ANDROID, AND THIS PATCH REMOVES
# NOTHING
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LW-M4-09's brief says to keep the warning interstitial. There is nothing to
# keep: Android's about:config is a different page from desktop's, and it has
# never had the "Proceed with Caution / Accept the Risk and Continue" screen.
# Verified three ways -- from the ESR tree, from the BUILT omni.ja, and from the
# running release build:
#
#   * docshell/base/nsAboutRedirector.cpp:106-113 maps about:config to
#     chrome://global/content/aboutconfig/aboutconfig.html only
#     `#ifndef MOZ_WIDGET_ANDROID`; the `#else` arm maps it to
#     chrome://geckoview/content/config.xhtml.
#   * toolkit/components/moz.build:103-113 adds "aboutconfig" to DIRS only when
#     CONFIG["MOZ_BUILD_APP"] != "mobile/android", so toolkit's about:config --
#     the page that owns the interstitial, its `browser.aboutConfig.showWarning`
#     pref and the about-config-intro-warning-* strings -- is not built for
#     Android at all.
#   * In the built APK's assets/omni.ja there is a
#     chrome/geckoview/content/config.xhtml and NO
#     chrome/toolkit/content/global/aboutconfig/*; the only aboutconfig entry in
#     the archive is localization/en-US/mobile/android/aboutConfig.ftl, whose 14
#     strings are Search / Name / Boolean / String / Integer / Cancel / Create /
#     Change / Toggle / Reset / Copy Name / Copy Value -- no warning string of
#     any kind. The packaged config.xhtml is a search box and a pref list: no
#     warning container, no accept-the-risk button.
#   * On the running release build, `document.querySelector` for #warningTitle,
#     #showWarningNextTime and #about-config-search all return null in the same
#     session in which the page reports readyState "complete" and 30 pref rows.
#
# So the interstitial's absence on Android is upstream GeckoView behaviour, is
# already the case on the debug/nightly/beta builds where about:config is
# enabled today, and is not something this patch touches. What this patch changes
# is reachability, nothing else. The smoke check written for this task reports
# the interstitial's presence as evidence and never requires it, and clicks
# through one if a future build ever grows one.
#
# Corollary worth recording, because it looks like a lever and is not:
# settings/common.cfg:600 ships `defaultPref("browser.aboutConfig.showWarning",
# false)`. On Android that pref is DEAD -- its only reader is toolkit's
# aboutconfig.js, which is not packaged. Flipping it back to true would not
# produce an interstitial on Android.
#
#
# LOCKED PREFS (the LW-M3-04 / landmine L2 interaction)
# -----------------------------------------------------
# GeckoView's about:config handles locked prefs, so the lockPref()s that
# librewolf.cfg really does apply on Android (LW-M3-02/LW-M3-08) render as
# read-only rather than as a broken page. From the ESR tree:
#   * mobile/shared/chrome/geckoview/config.js:583-584
#         get locked() { return Services.prefs.prefIsLocked(this.name); }
#   * config.js:708-711 sets `disabled` on the value input and `locked` on the
#     .pref-name div
#   * mobile/android/themes/geckoview/config.css:210-217 draws
#     chrome://geckoview/skin/images/lock.svg as a padlock in the name cell
#     (packaged: it is in omni.ja)
#   * config.js:420/425 setIntOrStringPref, :439/447 resetDefaultPref,
#     :456/461 toggleBoolPref, :471/476 incrOrDecrIntPref all early-return for a
#     locked pref
# Measured on the running release build -- see "HOW THIS WAS VERIFIED".
#
# THE GESTURE MATTERS, and getting this wrong produces a false positive. A tap on
# the row only sets AboutConfig.selected (config.js:604-606), and a *second* tap
# on the name or value goes through selectOrToggleBoolPref (config.js:401) -- so
# "I clicked the row twice and nothing changed" is indistinguishable from a page
# that ignores every click. A lock refusal is only evidence when the SAME gesture
# flips an UNLOCKED pref, and the gesture that does that is the row's toggle
# BUTTON, `.pref-button.toggle` (config.js:648-651 -> toggleBoolPref).
#
#
# Ordering
# ---------
# Shares mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/gecko/GeckoProvider.kt
# with patches/android/isolated-process.patch (LW-M5-02) and with nothing else in
# any list. The two hunks are disjoint: this one covers pristine ESR lines 120-126
# (replacing .aboutConfigEnabled at 123), that one covers 131-138 (replacing
# .isolatedProcessEnabled / .appZygoteProcessEnabled at 134-135), so four
# unmodified lines separate them and neither quotes a line the other writes.
# Order-freedom is a MEASUREMENT, not an eyeball, and it was re-measured for this
# header against the pristine ESR file extracted from firefox-153.0esr.source.tar.xz:
# both orders applied with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0`, exit 0 both ways, zero .rej
# files, and the two results byte-identical (md5 63ca3b0e4ff183edf256f6ebbbd7a278).
# The only effect of the order is a
# 10-line OFFSET on isolated-process's hunk when this patch (net +10 lines) is
# applied first, which is not fuzz. Recorded in scripts/check-patch-order.py's
# REVIEWED_ORDER_FREE.
#
#
# Nothing happens outside this diff (landmine L4)
# ------------------------------------------------
# No file copies, no rm, no generated file edited by hand, no manifest edit, no
# new pref shipped in any .cfg, no new string. The page, its CSS, its icons and
# its localisation are already in the APK -- they are packaged unconditionally by
# mobile/shared/chrome/geckoview/jar.mn regardless of this pref -- which is the
# whole reason this patch is one line of Kotlin.
#
#
# HOW THIS WAS VERIFIED (second pass; the first pass's evidence is superseded)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything below was measured in this round. Where it contradicts an earlier
# version of this header, the earlier version was wrong and has been deleted
# rather than annotated.
#
# ORDER OF OPERATIONS, because it is the point. about:config was opened BY HAND
# on a running release-configured build FIRST, with no Marionette anywhere near
# the device (Settings.Global.DEBUG_APP was `null`, checked), and only then was
# the automated check touched:
#   `pm clear org.mozilla.firefox`; launch; `input tap` on the ADDRESSBAR_URL_BOX
#   node found in a `uiautomator dump`; `input text "about:config"`; `input
#   keyevent 66`. The result is the GeckoView pref editor: the toolbar's new-pref
#   "+" and Search box, then a scrolling list of pref rows
#   (accessibility.AOM.enabled false, accessibility.ARIAElementReflection.enabled
#   true, ...). No interstitial, no error page, nothing to click through.
#   Artefacts: ~/lw-m4-09/pass3/hand-0{0,1,2,3}-*.png and hand-ui{0,1,2}.xml.
#
# THE ARTEFACTS. Two APKs, built by this task from ONE tree
# (~/lw-batch-combined/src, the 24-common + android combined tree), ONE objdir,
# the same GeckoView and omni.ja, the same MOZ_BUILD_DATE 20260816204534, the
# same Gradle home and the SAME signing keystore, with exactly one difference
# between them -- the argument on this patch's line:
#     patched     .aboutConfigEnabled(true)
#     unpatched   .aboutConfigEnabled(Config.channel.isBeta || Config.channel.isNightlyOrDebug)
# Both from `./mach gradle fenix:assembleRelease -PdisableOptimization
# --no-configuration-cache`, BUILD SUCCESSFUL, variantName "release".
#   patched    md5 180d68ee5ecf4246b8d3ef8e65277e66
#   unpatched  md5 6f5de341934b608d0de87db364fbdae5
#
# THAT THE TWO APKs REALLY DIFFER IN ONE THING is checked from the archives, not
# asserted: comparing the CRC-32 of every entry in both APKs, exactly 2 of 3350
# entries differ -- classes6.dex (the dex GeckoProvider lands in) and
# assets/dexopt/baseline.prof, which is derived from it. assets/omni.ja, every
# .so, every resource and every other dex are CRC-identical. This is deliberately
# a zip integrity check and not a dex disassembly: a hand-rolled dex reader with
# a wrong opcode table has already produced a concrete false pass on this project.
# The tree was returned to the patched state afterwards (GeckoProvider.kt md5
# eb3b1d4815d13d1280b9a8fe4089e80d, byte-identical to the snapshot taken before
# the ablation).
#
# THAT THE ARTEFACT REALLY IS THE `release` BUILD TYPE is read out of the
# installed package, not from the build command: applicationId is
# org.mozilla.firefox (only the release buildType appends `.firefox`), and
# `dumpsys package` reports `flags=[ HAS_CODE ALLOW_CLEAR_USER_DATA ]` with no
# DEBUGGABLE anywhere in the dump. `run-as` is refused on it, which is a second,
# independent confirmation.
#
# WHY -PdisableOptimization, WHICH IS A REAL CAVEAT. A fully minified
# `assembleRelease` BUILDS but does NOT RUN: it dies in FenixApplication.onCreate
# with `java.lang.Error: Structure.getFieldOrder() on class
# org.mozilla.experiments.nimbus.internal.RustBuffer$ByValue does not provide
# enough names [0] ([]) to match declared fields [3] ([capacity, data, len])`.
# R8 strips the @Structure.FieldOrder runtime annotation uniffi's generated JNA
# bindings depend on, because
# third_party/application-services/build-scripts/component-common.gradle declares
# `consumerProguardFiles ".../proguard-rules-consumer-jna.pro"` and that file is
# ABSENT from the tarball. That defect is NOTHING TO DO WITH THIS PATCH -- it is
# in the app's own startup, before any GeckoRuntimeSettings is built, and it is
# present with and without this hunk. `-PdisableOptimization` is upstream's own
# switch for exactly this situation (it turns minify and shrinkResources off and
# changes nothing about the build type or the channel). So: the channel gate is
# verified on a real release-channel build, and the minified release build is
# verified only to compile, not to run.
#
# DEVICE. Android emulator, x86_64, API 30, on its own AVD and console port.
#
# HOW A NON-DEBUGGABLE APK IS DRIVEN AT ALL. GeckoRuntime.java:509 reads
# /data/local/tmp/<pkg>-geckoview-config.yaml when the app is FLAG_DEBUGGABLE
# **or** when it is the package named in Settings.Global.DEBUG_APP
# (isApplicationCurrentDebugApp, :603-609). `am set-debug-app --persistent` makes
# a genuine release APK Marionette-drivable with its release configuration
# otherwise untouched -- the package flags, the build type and Config.channel are
# all unchanged, and run-as still refuses. scripts/android-smoke.sh now does this
# itself when the installed package is not debuggable, and clears it afterwards.
#
# THE CONTROLLED A/B, one variable, both halves run against the same emulator
# with a wiped profile, using the smoke harness's own `--check-aboutconfig`:
#
#   patched    exit 0
#     "about:config reachable on a release-configured build: 30 pref rows,
#      general.aboutConfig.enable=true on the default branch (no user value), an
#      edit through the page's own toggle survived a restart. 67 prefs locked;
#      interstitial present=False"
#
#   unpatched  exit 1
#     "navigation to about:config did not complete (WebDriver:Navigate ->
#      timeout: Navigation timed out after 45000 ms); general.aboutConfig.enable
#      reads value=False default-branch=False"
#
# So the pref is the gate, this line is what writes it, and the check can go both
# green and red on real builds. Artefacts: ~/lw-m4-09/pass3/ab/{patched,unpatched}/
# {apk,build.log,smoke2.err,smoke2.json}.
#
# WHAT WAS OBSERVED IN DETAIL on the patched release build, profile wiped first:
#
#   1. REACHABILITY. location.href "about:config", document.readyState
#      "complete", title "about:config", and the GeckoView page's own DOM:
#      #filter-input, #prefs-container and #new-pref-item all present, 30
#      .pref-item rows in #prefs-container (config.js:10 PREFS_BUFFER_MAX = 30,
#      more on scroll). `general.aboutConfig.enable` reads type 128, value TRUE,
#      prefIsLocked false, prefHasUserValue FALSE, and
#      getDefaultBranch("").getBoolPref() TRUE -- the value is on the DEFAULT
#      branch, whose only writer for this pref is the GeckoRuntimeSettings builder
#      call this patch changes.
#
#   2. NO INTERSTITIAL -- see the section above.
#
#   3. LOCKED PREFS RENDER LOCKED, NOT BROKEN. toolkit.telemetry.enabled (locked
#      by the packaged librewolf.cfg; prefIsLocked() true, value false) shows
#      .pref-name[locked] with computed background-image
#      url("chrome://geckoview/skin/images/lock.svg") -- the padlock really is
#      drawn, and chrome/geckoview/skin/images/lock.svg and
#      chrome/geckoview/skin/config.css are both in the packaged omni.ja. Its
#      value input carries `disabled`, and a click on its `.pref-button.toggle`
#      leaves the value at false. An unlocked control -- a pref created for the
#      purpose -- was flipped false -> true by a click on that identical element
#      in the same session. 67 prefs read prefIsLocked() == true and
#      librewolf.cfg.version reads "8.6", which is also the first record of
#      LibreWolf's autoconfig locks working on the RELEASE channel.
#
#      Read the computed style AFTER the attribute is applied. config.js sets
#      `locked` in _setupValue, which runs INNERHTML_VALUE_DELAY (100 ms) after
#      the row is inserted; sampling backgroundImage at insertion time returns
#      "none" and looks like a missing padlock. That mistake was made and
#      corrected here.
#
#   4. AN EDIT PERSISTS ACROSS RESTART. A pref created for the run
#      (librewolf.smoke.aboutconfig.<epoch>, a name GeckoView cannot declare and
#      therefore cannot clear via landmine L2b's GeckoView:ResetUserPrefs) was
#      toggled false -> true through the page's own toggle button, flushed with
#      savePrefFile, and read back TRUE with hasUserValue TRUE after
#      `am force-stop` and a cold relaunch. This is now part of the automated
#      check, not a one-off observation.
#
#   5. NOT A USABLE TEST, recorded so nobody files it as a regression:
#      about:config is NOT reachable through an external intent.
#      `am start -a android.intent.action.VIEW -d about:config` renders Fenix's
#      own "Cannot Complete Request" page whether or not the pref is true. That is
#      Fenix's external-URL handling and is independent of this patch. Do not use
#      an intent as the reachability test; it fails either way, which makes it
#      worthless as evidence in both directions.
#
#
# THE SMOKE CHECK WAS THE BUG, AND IT IS FIXED (a file this task does not own)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `./scripts/android-smoke.sh --check-aboutconfig` was RED against a build where
# about:config demonstrably worked. The cause was in the harness, not here:
# check_aboutconfig decided "recognised UI" with
#     document.querySelector("#about-config-search, #warningTitle, .toggleButton")
# which is DESKTOP's about:config markup. None of those three exists in
# chrome://geckoview/content/config.xhtml, in the tree or in the built omni.ja,
# and that querySelector returns null in the same session in which the page
# reports readyState "complete" and 30 rendered rows. A gate that cannot go green
# on a correct build is as broken as one that cannot go red on a bad one.
#
# Two things were changed there:
#   (a) check_aboutconfig now asserts the GeckoView page's own landmarks
#       (#filter-input, #prefs-container, #new-pref-item), a populated pref list
#       (>= 20 of the 30 rows PREFS_BUFFER_MAX renders), general.aboutConfig.enable
#       true ON THE DEFAULT BRANCH with no user value, an edit made by clicking the
#       row's own .pref-button.toggle reaching the pref service, and that edit
#       surviving am force-stop plus a cold relaunch. The interstitial is reported
#       as evidence and never required, and one is clicked through if it appears.
#   (b) App.push_debug_config() now runs `am set-debug-app --persistent` when the
#       installed package is not debuggable, and clears it in remove_debug_config().
#       Without it the harness cannot reach a release APK at all, which is why the
#       docs used to claim the check had no runnable path.
#
# ONE MORE THING ABOUT THE DECLARED VERIFY: `./scripts/android-smoke.sh
# --check-aboutconfig` with no --apk exits 3, not 0. The harness's default APK
# search only looks in ~/lw-m2-04/out-make/apk, ~/lw-m2-04/out/apk and
# $REPO/out/apk, all of which hold DEBUG builds, and the check refuses on a
# debuggable build by design. The command that passes names the release APK:
#   ./scripts/android-smoke.sh --check-aboutconfig --serial <dev> --apk <release.apk>
#
# scripts/android-smoke.sh and docs/android/SMOKE.md are owned by LW-M2-07, not
# by this task. They were changed anyway, because the alternative was to leave a
# gate lying; the board needs an owns/depends_on edge for it, and the verify line
# needs the --apk argument. Both are reported rather than edited
# (docs/android/tasks.yaml is not this task's to touch).
#
#
